#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079268} :activity/id #uuid "128934d0-aa66-5625-aab3-6d772fa10846" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050643 :topic/id #uuid "5ba94c26-75d7-5806-9fad-73f65947835b" :topic/math-academy-id 637} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079268 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079652 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wish to calculate the limit\n\n$$\nL=\\lim\\limits_{h\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\ln(2+h) -\\ln(2)}{h}\n$$\n\nThe key here is to notice that the limit looks very similar to the instantaneous rate of change formula,\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim\\limits_{h\\to 0} \\dfrac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h}\n$$\n\nfor the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\ln x\n$$\n\nat $a=2$.\n\nLet's substitute\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\ln x\n$$\n\nand $a=2$ into the above, just to be sure:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(2) &= lim_(h → 0)(f(2 + h) - f(2))/(h) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)(\\ln (2 + h) - \\ln (2))/(h) \\\\\n&= L\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis confirms that $L = f'(2)$. So instead of working out that difficult limit, we only need to work out $f'(2)$.\n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\frac{1}{x}, \\qquad f'(2) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that\n\n$$\nL=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "db8ca187-38da-5852-8df8-cd1cb0f754cc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2631 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3b7e08e0-d936-5e59-996f-679f185c3562" :step/math-academy-id 12352 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079269}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079269 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078407 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078279 :question/id #uuid "5f4f75ce-d8df-5970-bffe-99beb9d9aac9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3650"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f5c089a-9506-5a0e-83ae-48efa8fd314c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234076}] :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Derivatives in Limits Similar to the Instantaneous Rate of Change Formula"} :step/id #uuid "cca4127e-8b00-5649-abdf-3f0477315c35" :step/math-academy-id 12353 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079270}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079270 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079653 :tutorial/content "We can apply the same method for calculating limits that are similar to the equivalent definition of the derivative,\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim\\limits_{x\\to a} \\dfrac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a}\n$$\n\nIt is important to choose the correct form for each limit. If the numerator of the limit looks like $f(a+h) -f(a)$ for some $a$ and the denominator looks like $h$, then use the form\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{h\\to 0} \\dfrac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}\n$$\n\nBut if the numerator looks like $f(x)-f(a)$ and the denominator looks like $x-a$ for some $a$, then use the form\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to a} \\dfrac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "88b647b7-8adf-517b-848b-5323c6565ced" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2632 :tutorial/title "Recognizing the Equivalent Definition of the Derivative in Limits"} :step/id #uuid "a1fec940-4ec4-58c8-8553-9ddb7fd70bff" :step/math-academy-id 12354 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079271}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079271 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078408 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078280 :question/id #uuid "3b5966f8-c23f-5ef1-8e5c-7862a020402d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3651"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b613bf7-a641-547f-8487-d6af0da0f724" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048465}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079003}] :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Derivatives in Limits Similar to the Equivalent Definition of the Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "4bbfd52a-c53b-5698-a9e3-0f3cadd9059f" :step/math-academy-id 12355 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079272}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079272 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078409 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078281 :question/id #uuid "4a42aea1-4927-5d6e-af94-d43b834e295a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3652"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20df0d0d-a94e-55e6-8d19-25a566ab794d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079005}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Limits Equivalent to a Derivative Using a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "6d9b76a5-30f4-5ef3-8887-46953ed35bc3" :step/math-academy-id 12356}] :activity/title "Recognizing Derivatives in Limits" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078660}]}
